[[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], as a [[Premier League]] club,{{efn|Premier League and Championship clubs entered the FA Cup competition in the third round.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thefa.com/-/media/thefacom-new/files/competitions/2019-20/fa-cup/the-fa-cup-rules-2019-20.ashx|publisher=[[The Football Association]]|access-date=20 August 2021|title=Rules of the FA Challenge Cup Competition ("the Competition")|format=pdf|page=3|archive-date=24 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210524151832/https://www.thefa.com/-/media/thefacom-new/files/competitions/2019-20/fa-cup/the-fa-cup-rules-2019-20.ashx|url-status=live}}</ref>}} started their [[2019–20 FA Cup]] campaign in the third round at their home ground, the [[Emirates Stadium]], against [[EFL Championship]] side [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]]. After a goalless first half that Leeds dominated with 15 shots, including a [[Patrick Bamford]] strike that hit the crossbar, Arsenal took the lead in the 55th minute when [[Reiss Nelson]] scored from a deflected [[Alexandre Lacazette]] shot. Arsenal hit the Leeds crossbar from a Lacazette [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] and the match ended 1–0 to the home side.<ref>{{cite news |first=Emlyn |last=Begley |title=Arsenal 1–0 Leeds United: Reiss Nelson goal sends Gunners into fourth round |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50976532 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=6 January 2020 |access-date=18 July 2020 |archive-date=28 January 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200128164818/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50976532 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the fourth round, Arsenal played Premier League side [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]] away at [[Dean Court]]. Arsenal took an early lead when [[Bukayo Saka]] struck the ball past Bournemouth goalkeeper [[Mark Travers]] in the fifth minute. [[Eddie Nketiah]] doubled the lead in the 21 minutes later and although [[Sam Surridge]] scored in second-half [[stoppage time]] for Bournemouth, Arsenal won 2–1.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Bournemouth 1–2 Arsenal: Arteta pleased with 'courageous' Gunners in FA Cup win |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51208535 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=27 January 2020 |access-date=18 July 2020 |archive-date=3 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200203183532/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51208535 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the next round, Arsenal were drawn away to [[EFL League One|League One]] side [[Portsmouth F.C.|Portsmouth]] who went into the game at [[Fratton Park]] on a ten-match [[winning streak]]. The first half remained goalless until four minutes into stoppage time when [[Sokratis Papastathopoulos|Sokratis]] scored with a [[Volley (association football)|volley]] from a [[Cross (association football)|cross]] from Nelson. Six minutes into the second half, Nketiah scored a close-range goal from Nelson's cross; the game ended 2–0 to Arsenal.<ref>{{cite news |first=Emlyn |last=Begley |title=Portsmouth 0–2 Arsenal: Gunners ease into FA Cup quarter-finals |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51697793 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=2 March 2020 |access-date=18 July 2020 |archive-date=17 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200917181705/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51697793 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the quarter-finals, Arsenal faced fellow Premier League side [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]] away at [[Bramall Lane]]. In the 25th minute, Lacazette was fouled by [[Chris Basham]] in Sheffield United's [[penalty area]]; [[Nicolas Pépé]] converted the subsequent [[penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]]. [[David McGoldrick]] equalised three minutes before the end of regular time, striking the ball into Arsenal's net following a long [[throw-in]]. One minute into stoppage time, [[Dani Ceballos]] gathered a loose ball in Sheffield United's box and struck a low shot beneath [[Dean Henderson]] to give Arsenal a 2–1 victory.<ref>{{cite news |title=Sheffield United 1–2 Arsenal: Dani Ceballos' late goal sends Arsenal to FA Cup semis |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53139211 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 June 2020 |access-date=18 July 2020 |archive-date=10 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201110000909/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53139211 |url-status=live }}</ref> In their semi-final, played at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London (a [[Glossary of association football terms#N|neutral venue]]), Arsenal played Premier League side [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]], the [[2019 FA Cup final|FA Cup holders]]. [[Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang]] put Arsenal ahead in the 19th minute when he scored from Pépé's cross with the outside of his foot. He then scored another in the 71st minute to secure a 2–0 victory and progression to the FA Cup final.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Arsenal 2–0 Man City: Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang guides Gunners into FA Cup final |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53419787 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=18 July 2020 |access-date=18 July 2020 |archive-date=16 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210116090933/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53419787 |url-status=live }}</ref>

