[[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] started their FA Cup run in the third round with a home game at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] against Championship side [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]]. [[Callum Hudson-Odoi]] scored after six minutes to give Chelsea the lead. [[Ross Barkley]] then struck from close range in the 33rd minute after another Hudson-Odoi shot was saved. The second half was goalless and Chelsea won the match 2–0.<ref>{{cite web |first=Michael |last=Emons |title=Chelsea 2–0 Nottingham Forest, FA Cup third round |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50976149 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=5 January 2020 |access-date=19 July 2020 |archive-date=28 January 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200128164458/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50976149 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the fourth round, they were drawn against another Championship side, [[Hull City A.F.C.|Hull City]], this time away at the [[KCOM Stadium]]. [[Michy Batshuayi]] put the visitors ahead with a shot which deflected off [[Ryan Tafazolli]] past Hull City goalkeeper [[George Long (footballer)|George Long]]. [[Fikayo Tomori]] scored with a [[Header (association football)|header]] from a Barkley free kick, making it 2–0. With 12 minutes remaining, [[Kamil Grosicki]] scored for Hull City with a deflected free kick but Chelsea progressed with a 2–1 win.<ref>{{cite news |first=Gary |last=Rose |title=Hull City 1–2 Chelsea: Blues edge Tigers to reach FA Cup fifth round |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51208507 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=25 January 2020 |access-date=19 July 2020 |archive-date=3 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200203113503/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51208507 |url-status=live }}</ref> Chelsea faced fifth-round opponents [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], a Premier League club, at home. [[Willian (footballer, born 1988)|Willian]] put the home side ahead after 13 minutes, when Liverpool goalkeeper [[Adrián (footballer)|Adrián]] failed to gather his initial {{convert|20|yd}} shot. Midway through the second half, Barkley scored following a solo run, giving Chelsea a 2–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Chelsea 2–0 Liverpool: Reds suffer second successive defeat |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51698397 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=3 March 2020 |access-date=19 July 2020 |archive-date=31 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201231210956/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51698397 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the quarter-finals, Chelsea were drawn away against Premier League side [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] at the [[King Power Stadium]]. After a goalless first half dominated by the home team, second-half [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]] Barkley scored from Willian's cross to secure a 1–0 Chelsea victory.<ref>{{cite news |authorlink=Phil McNulty |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Leicester City 0–1 Chelsea: Ross Barkley goal takes Blues into FA Cup semi-finals |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53139218 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 June 2020 |access-date=19 July 2020 |archive-date=17 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210117223714/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53139218 |url-status=live }}</ref> Chelsea's semi-final with Premier League side [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] took place at Wembley Stadium; the sides were meeting in the FA Cup for the fourth consecutive season.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Manchester%20United/ |website=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=20 May 2021 |title=Chelsea football club: record v Manchester United |archive-date=8 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210608195230/https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Manchester%20United/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Olivier Giroud]] gave Chelsea the lead 11 minutes into first-half stoppage time after a mistake from Manchester United goalkeeper [[David de Gea]]. A minute after half time, [[Mason Mount]] made it 2–0 when De Gea fumbled Mount's shot from {{convert|20|yd}}. [[Harry Maguire]] scored an [[own goal]] in the 74th minute when he deflected a cross from [[Marcos Alonso (footballer, born 1990)|Marcos Alonso]]. With five minutes remaining, Manchester United's [[Anthony Martial]] was fouled by Hudson-Odoi in Chelsea's penalty area; [[Bruno Fernandes]] scored the penalty. Chelsea won 3–1 and progressed to the final.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Manchester United 1–3 Chelsea: De Gea errors help settle FA Cup semi-final |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53420073 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=19 July 2020 |access-date=19 July 2020 |archive-date=19 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200719201851/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53420073 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==

===Background===
The two finalists share a [[Arsenal F.C.–Chelsea F.C. rivalry|London derby rivalry]], with the sides having faced each other more than 200 times since their inaugural match in November 1907.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Arsenal/ |website=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=9 August 2021 |title=Chelsea football club: record v Arsenal |archive-date=30 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210130022709/https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Arsenal/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The final, the 21st FA Cup match between the sides, was a repeat of the [[2002 FA Cup final|2002]] and [[2017 FA Cup final|2017]] FA Cup finals, both of which Arsenal had won.<ref name=thefa/><ref name=bbc13>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=13 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (13 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=6 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201006003837/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790/page/13 |url-status=live }}</ref> It was also a repeat of the [[2019 UEFA Europa League Final|previous year's UEFA Europa League final]], which Chelsea won.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48368406 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=26 April 2021 |first=Chris |last=Bevan |date=29 May 2019 |title=Chelsea beat Arsenal 4–1 to win Europe League final |archive-date=17 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190617224721/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48368406 |url-status=live }}</ref> While playing for the clubs they now [[Manager (association football)|managed]], [[Mikel Arteta]] of Arsenal had won two FA Cups; [[Frank Lampard]], Chelsea's manager, had won four.<ref>{{Cite web |url =https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53593395 |first =Phil |last =McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |access-date=26 April 2021 |date=31 July 2020 |title =FA Cup final 2020: Why Arsenal's Mikel Arteta and Chelsea's Frank Lampard have a bright future |publisher =[[BBC Sport]] |archive-date =25 February 2021 |archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20210225023851/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53593395 |url-status =live }}</ref> The 2020 FA Cup Final was the 139th final of the competition overall.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.thefa.com/news/2020/jul/21/heads-up-fa-cup-final-kickoff-confirmed-210720 |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |access-date=20 August 2021 |title=Details of the Heads Up FA Cup Final at Wembley on Saturday 1 August are finalised |date=22 July 2020 |archive-date=26 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210226213136/https://www.thefa.com/news/2020/jul/21/heads-up-fa-cup-final-kickoff-confirmed-210720 |url-status=live }}</ref>

This was Chelsea's 15th FA Cup final, their last appearance being in [[2018 FA Cup final|2018]] where the secured a 1–0 win over Manchester United, and Arsenal's 21st, their most recent being a 2–1 victory over Chelsea in 2017. Before the 2020 final, Arsenal's held the record for most FA Cup wins with 13.<ref name=thefa>{{Cite web |url=https://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/stories/fa-cup-finals |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |title=FA Cup Finals – the winner's list |access-date=26 April 2021 |archive-date=10 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210410202646/https://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/stories/fa-cup-finals |url-status=live }}</ref> In the two matches between the clubs during the [[2019–20 Premier League]], Chelsea won 2–1 at the Emirates Stadium in December 2019 while the game at Stamford Bridge the following month ended in a 2–2 draw.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Arsenal/ |website=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=26 April 2021 |title=Chelsea football club: record v Arsenal |archive-date=30 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210130022709/https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Arsenal/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The Premier League season ended with Chelsea in fourth position and Arsenal eighth, ten points behind.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/leagueTables/2020/ |website=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=26 April 2021 |title=Premier League table at close of 2019–20 season |archive-date=26 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210426165723/https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/leagueTables/2020/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Arsenal's final league position meant that winning the FA Cup was the club's only means of qualifying for European football the following season.<ref name=bbc13/> Both sides had lost in the [[EFL Cup]]'s fourth round; Arsenal lost away to Liverpool in a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] after their match at [[Anfield]] ended 5–5, while Chelsea lost 2–1 at home to Manchester United.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/49937966 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=26 April 2021 |title=Liverpool win on penalties in epic, Rashford gives Man Utd victory |date=30 October 2019 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=7 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210307222715/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/49937966 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Originally scheduled for 23 May, the final was delayed due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United Kingdom]] and was played on 1 August 2020 [[Behind closed doors (sport)|behind closed doors]] at Wembley Stadium.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup final to be held on 1 August with quarter-finals resuming on 27 June |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2020/may/29/fa-cup-final-to-be-held-on-1-august-with-quarter-finals-resuming-on-27-june-football |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=29 May 2020 |access-date=17 July 2020 |archive-date=17 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200717075139/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2020/may/29/fa-cup-final-to-be-held-on-1-august-with-quarter-finals-resuming-on-27-june-football |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Watch: 2006 FA Cup Final Rewind – Liverpool win FA Cup on penalties |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/52726943 |first=Neil |last=Johnston |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=23 May 2020 |access-date=9 September 2020 |archive-date=13 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200613143834/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/52726943 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=bcd /> The [[Referee (association football)|referee]] was [[Anthony Taylor (referee)|Anthony Taylor]], representing the [[Cheshire Football Association]].<ref name="referees">{{cite news |title=Referee Anthony Taylor and his team will take charge of the Heads Up FA Cup Final |url=http://www.thefa.com/news/2020/jul/22/heads-up-fa-cup-final-match-officials-confirmed-220720 |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=22 July 2020 |access-date=22 July 2020 |archive-date=23 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200723090429/http://www.thefa.com/news/2020/jul/22/heads-up-fa-cup-final-match-officials-confirmed-220720 |url-status=live }}</ref> Having refereed 2017's final, he became the first person since [[Arthur Kingscott]] in [[1901 FA Cup final|1901]] to referee two finals. Ordinarily referees would only officiate one FA Cup final, but with the 2020 final taking place without supporters in the stadium, the FA appointed Taylor for a second time. [[David Elleray]], the FA Referees' Committee chairman, said "a significant part of the Cup Final appointment is the opportunity to share this – the English refereeing pinnacle – with partners, family, friends and those who have been an important part of their long journey to the Final{{nbsp}}... Sadly, this year's Final will be very different and will be held without all these elements in an empty stadium. With this in mind, the Committee decided it would be unfair to appoint someone who has not yet done the Final and have, instead, appointed Anthony Taylor to his second FA Cup Final."<ref name="referees"/><ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup Final: Anthony Taylor to referee second final |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53504001 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=1 August 2020 |date=22 July 2020 |archive-date=27 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200727045625/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53504001 |url-status=live }}</ref> Taylor was [[Assistant referee (association football)|assisted]] by Gary Beswick and Adam Nunn. [[Chris Kavanagh (referee)|Chris Kavanagh]] was the fourth official and Lee Betts was the reserve assistant referee. [[Stuart Attwell]] was the [[video assistant referee]] (VAR).<ref name="referees"/>

Chelsea's starting lineup was unchanged from their previous match, a league game against [[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]]. Arsenal made one change to the side which started at their previous game, against [[Watford F.C.|Watford]] in the Premier League, [[Héctor Bellerín]] replacing [[Joe Willock]].<ref name=bbc11>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=11 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (11 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=21 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210721140026/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790/page/11 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53460584 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=26 July 2020 |first=Neil |last=Johnston |title=Arsenal 3–2 Watford: Hornets relegated from Premier League |archive-date=26 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210126231325/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53460584 |url-status=live }}</ref> Both sides adopted a [[Formation (association football)#3–4–3|3–4–3 formation]] and Aubameyang [[Captain (association football)|captained]] Arsenal while [[César Azpilicueta]] was Chelsea's captain.<ref name=bbc11/>

The 2020 FA Cup Final was officially named the "Heads Up FA Cup Final" by [[the Football Association]] as part of [[The Royal Foundation#Heads Together|a campaign around mental health awareness]] promoted by The FA president, [[Prince William, Duke of Cambridge]].<ref name=heads>{{cite web |title=2020 FA Cup final renamed Heads Up FA Cup final to promote mental health |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53015666 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=11 June 2020 |access-date=17 July 2020 |archive-date=28 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200628070950/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53015666 |url-status=live }}</ref> Before the match, a recording of [[Emeli Sandé]] singing the traditional hymn "[[Abide with Me]]" was broadcast from Wembley's roof, and within the stadium the [[British Youth Opera]], accompanied by the [[Bands of the Household Division (United Kingdom)|Massed Bands of the Household Division]], performed the English national anthem "[[God Save the Queen]]".<ref name=bbc9>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=9 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (9 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=21 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210721140022/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790/page/9 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Summary===
====First half====
[[File:USMNT vs. Trinidad and Tobago (48125059622) (cropped).jpg|right|thumb|upright|alt=Christian Pulisic|Christian Pulisic ''(pictured in 2019)'' scored the opening goal of the final for Chelsea.]]
The match [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] at 5:30{{nbsp}}p.m. and the first chance to score fell to Arsenal: in the third minute, [[Ainsley Maitland-Niles]] played in a cross which Aubameyang headed wide. A minute later, Mount struck a shot from around {{convert|20|yd}} which [[Emiliano Martínez]], the Arsenal goalkeeper, dived to push away. In the fifth minute, Chelsea took the lead through [[Christian Pulisic]]. From a midfield position, he passed the ball to Mount, who took it into Arsenal's penalty area. Mount passed to Giroud; he passed back to Pulisic who scored from close range, becoming the first player from the United States to score in an FA Cup final. Three minutes later, Pépé had a shot from distance saved by Chelsea goalkeeper [[Willy Caballero]]. Pulisic then evaded [[Rob Holding]] before striking a shot which was saved by Martínez.<ref name=bbc9/> [[Mateo Kovačić]] became the game's first player to be shown a [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] when, in the 13th minute, he fouled [[Granit Xhaka]]. Ceballos then struck a free kick too high before the match was paused for a drinks interval.<ref name=bbc8>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=8 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (8 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=21 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210721140025/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790/page/8 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Two minutes after the restart, Pépé's strike from around {{convert|20|yd}} flew into the top corner of Chelsea's net but the goal was disallowed as Aubameyang was [[Offside (association football)|offside]] during the build-up. In the 28th minute, Azpilicueta brought Aubameyang down as he ran in on Chelsea's goal.<ref name=bbc8/> The Chelsea player was booked and Arsenal were awarded a penalty, which Aubameyang took, beating Caballero who dived in the wrong direction, making the score 1–1. Azpilicueta then went down with a leg injury and was replaced by [[Andreas Christensen]] in the game's first substitution. With eight minutes remaining in the first half, Mount crossed the ball from the left to [[Jorginho (footballer, born December 1991)|Jorginho]] but his shot was well over Arsenal's crossbar as Alonso inadvertently got in his way. Following Christensen tripping Pépé in the 44th minute, Arsenal's players appealed for a penalty, but it was deemed to have occurred outside the penalty area.<ref name=bbc7>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=7 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (7 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=27 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210427203458/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/53257790/page/7 |url-status=live }}</ref> Lacazette struck the resulting free kick wide of Chelsea's goal. The half ended 1–1.<ref name=bbc6>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=6 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (6 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=27 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210427203449/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/53257790/page/6 |url-status=live }}</ref>

====Second half====
Neither side made any substitutions during the interval.<ref name=bbcrep/> Straight from the kick-off, Pulisic [[dribbling|dribbled]] past Arsenal's defence but his shot went wide. It was his final contribution as he suffered a hamstring injury and was replaced by [[Pedro (footballer, born 1987)|Pedro]]. Chelsea dominated the second half's early stages and in the 62nd minute Pedro passed to [[Reece James (footballer, born 1999)|Reece James]], but James's shot was off-target.<ref name=bbc5>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=5 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (5 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=27 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210427203453/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/53257790/page/5 |url-status=live }}</ref> Three minutes later, Mount went clear down the left wing and crossed but none of his teammates were in a position to convert the opportunity. Midway through the second half, Arsenal took the lead. Bellerin ran through the middle with the ball but was [[Tackle (football move)#Association football|tackled]] by Christensen. Pépé collected the loose ball and passed to Aubameyang on the left side of Chelsea's penalty area. Aubameyang ran past Chelsea defender [[Kurt Zouma]] before lifting the ball over Caballero to make it 2–1 in the 67th minute. The second-half drinks break was then taken.<ref name=bbc4>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=4 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (4 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=27 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210427203431/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/53257790/page/4 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the 73rd minute, Chelsea were reduced to 10 men when Kovačić was [[sent off]] after fouling Xhaka and being shown a second yellow card. With 12 minutes remaining, Chelsea made a triple-substitution: Barkley, Hudson-Odoi and [[Tammy Abraham]] came on for Mount, [[Antonio Rüdiger]] and Giroud. Two minutes later, Arsenal sent on Nketiah to replace Lacazette,<ref name=bbc4/> before [[David Luiz]] withdrew with a leg injury and Sokratis replaced him. With a minute of regular time remaining, Barkley was booked and the match went into seven minutes of stoppage time. A corner from Pedro was gathered by Martinez before Pedro was attended to by medical staff for an injured shoulder.<ref name=bbc3>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=3 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (3 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=8 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108170644/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790/page/3 |url-status=live }}</ref> After several minutes of attention, he left the pitch on a stretcher. After 14 minutes of stoppage time, the game was brought to an end. Arsenal won 2–1, claiming a record 14th FA Cup.<ref name=bbc2>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790?page=2 |title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (2 of 13) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=27 April 2021 |date=1 August 2020 |first=Tom |last=Rostance |archive-date=5 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201005001837/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790/page/2 |url-status=live }}</ref>

