==Post-match==
As part of precautions against COVID-19, Arsenal received the trophy on the pitch and not, as in previous seasons, by climbing steps to the Royal Box for the presentation.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup final: Winners to collect trophy on Wembley pitch |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53591462 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=30 July 2020 |access-date=1 August 2020 |archive-date=28 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200928061406/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53591462 |url-status=live }}</ref> Whilst carrying the trophy to his team before its formal lifting, Aubameyang dropped the cup and the base.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/av/football/53625491 |title=Watch: Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang drops{{nbsp}}... then lifts the FA Cup after Arsenal victory |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=1 August 2020 |access-date=26 April 2021 |archive-date=15 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210215201903/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/av/football/53625491 |url-status=live }}</ref> Aubameyang was named [[Player of the match|man of the match]].<ref name=bbcrep/> The game was broadcast live in the United Kingdom on [[BT Sport]] and [[BBC]], with BBC's coverage watched by 8.2&nbsp;million viewers, making it the season's most watched football match in the UK.<ref>{{cite web|date=2 August 2020|title=FA Cup final on BBC sets viewing record for 2019–20 season with 8.2m|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53628532|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200805144945/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53628532|archive-date=5 August 2020|website=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=16 August 2020}}</ref>

Arteta noted his side had made a slow start but their subsequent performance was impressive: "It was a difficult start when we conceded so early{{nbsp}}... We played probably the best 30 minutes after since I arrived. I am so proud to represent these players and this club."<ref name="bbc">{{Cite web|first=Tom|last=Rostance|date=1 August 2020|title=Watch: Arsenal beat Chelsea to win FA Cup final – reaction (1 of 13)|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201211054504/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/53257790|archive-date=11 December 2020|website=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=27 April 2021}}</ref> His counterpart Lampard agreed Chelsea had started strongly but then "got complacent, we took time on the ball like it was a stroll and we allowed them into the game."<ref name=bbc/> 

Former [[England national football team|England]] and [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] striker [[Alan Shearer]] suggested Arsenal were worthy winners and "showed great character after going behind", though noted that "Pulisic going off injured helped them."<ref name="bbc2" /> [[Ashley Cole]], who had played for both Arsenal and Chelsea, agreed, saying "Arsenal did deserve it. Mikel Arteta has done a great job."<ref name="bbc2" /> Ex-Arsenal forward [[Ian Wright]] suggested Chelsea had been unlucky: "Chelsea were unfortunate with those injuries and they had some decisions which were very unfair on them."<ref name="bbc2" />

As winners, Arsenal entered the [[2020–21 UEFA Europa League group stage|UEFA Europa League group stage]] and also qualified to play Liverpool in the [[2020 FA Community Shield|FA Community Shield]] at the end of the month, winning the latter 5–4 on penalties after a 1–1 draw in 120 minutes of regulation-extended time.<ref>{{Cite web|first=Emma|last=Sanders|date=29 August 2020|title=Arsenal 1–1 (5–4 pens) Liverpool|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53871874|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210324193022/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53871874|archive-date=24 March 2021|website=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=27 April 2021}}</ref>

